One way to explain this assignment is as follows. Field_2 has the highest threat (0.19) and already requires 4 drones for full protection, so we keep four drones there (Drone_1, Drone_4, Drone_5, and Drone_8). Next, Field_3 (threat 0.15) needs 2 drones for full coverage – we keep the drone already moving toward Field_3 (Drone_3) and “steal” one drone from the lower‐threat Field_4 (Drone_2) to complete the pair. Finally, because we only have 8 drones in total, the two drones originally guarding Field_4 (Drone_6 and Drone_7) are re‐allocated to Field_1 (threat 0.12) even though Field_1 requires 4 drones for full protection; having 2 will provide partial protection that, while not ideal, is preferable to leaving a higher‐threat field (Field_3) partially or unprotected. Field_4 (threat 0.07) is left without any drone protection as it is the lowest priority in this situation.
